  "text": "Idea 1: Pay attention to bids for connection. When she shares a detail, asks a question, or makes a comment, it's a bid for your attention or interest. Picking up on these small moments builds rapport.\nSituational context: Responding to her messages. \nIn a message: Instead of just answering her question, acknowledge the detail she shared. For example, if she mentions a movie, ask about her favorite part of it, rather than just saying 'I like movies too.' \nExample: 'Sunday markets and a flat white sounds like a perfect Sunday.'\n\nIdea 2: Turn towards her bids for connection. This means actively responding positively when she reaches out, rather than ignoring, dismissing, or turning away. This builds trust and shows you're engaged.\nSituational context: When she initiates contact or shares something. \nIn a message: If she sends a short message or a funny observation, respond with genuine interest and enthusiasm. Avoid brief, dismissive replies that might signal disinterest.\nExample: 'Haha, I can totally picture that! What happened next?'\n\nIdea 3: Small positive moments build connection over time. Consistent, small positive interactions are more impactful than grand gestures. Showing consistent interest and engagement in everyday conversation strengthens the bond.\nSituational context: Throughout the early stages of texting. \nIn a message: Regularly engage with her updates and comments, even if they seem minor. Show that you're paying attention and that these small interactions matter to you.\nExample: 'Glad you had a good time at the concert! What was the best song they played?'\n\nIdea 4: Listen for feelings, not just facts. When she talks about her day or experiences, try to understand the emotional undercurrent. Responding to the emotion she conveys, not just the event itself, creates deeper connection. Keep it to one short, light line, not a long comfort speech.\nSituational context: When she's describing an event or her day.\nIn a message: Validate the feeling she expresses. If she says she's tired after a long day, acknowledge that feeling instead of just asking what she did.\nExample: 'That sounds exhausting! Hope you can relax tonight.'\n\nIdea 5: Create curiosity by asking open-ended questions. Instead of asking yes/no questions, ask questions that invite her to share more about herself, her thoughts, and her experiences. This encourages deeper conversation. Ask one question at a time and react to her answer, so it never feels like an interview.\nSituational context: When trying to learn more about her.\nIn a message: Ask questions that can't be answered with a single word. For example, instead of 'Did you like the book?', ask 'What did you think of the book's ending?'\nExample: 'What's the most interesting thing you learned this week?'\n\nIn practice: Focus on picking up on her cues, responding warmly and with genuine interest to her messages. Show you're present and engaged by asking thoughtful questions and acknowledging her feelings, creating small positive moments that build rapport."
